      ":question/problem": "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}_{0}^{\u2032}(t)=A{\u03a6}_{0}(t),\\,{\u03a6}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={\u03a6}_{0}(0)\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${\u03a6}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the definition of ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)$ as the standard fundamental matrix by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}."
